<BaseLayout title="About — Young Systems" description="About Samuel Young and the thinking behind Young Systems.">
  <header class="page-header"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Engineering with an operator’s perspective.</h1><p>I’m Samuel Young, a systems engineer focused on Microsoft cloud, infrastructure, identity, and automation.</p></header>
  <div class="content-split">
    <aside>Profile / 01</aside>
    <div class="content-body">
      <h2>I care about what happens after deployment.</h2>
      <p>Good infrastructure should be understandable, supportable, secure, and easier to operate six months after deployment. My work sits between architecture and day-to-day operations.</p>
      <p>I work across Microsoft 365, migrations, identity, compliance, and integration. My homelab extends that work into virtualization, networking, containers, storage, and identity-aware access.</p>
      <h2>Why Young Systems exists</h2>
      <p>This site is my working technical record: a place to publish useful automation, document infrastructure projects, and explain decisions with enough context to help another engineer. It is a portfolio of the work itself—not a wall of marketing copy.</p>
      <div class="principles">
        <div class="principle"><span>01</span><div><h3>Make it repeatable</h3><p>Manual work is sometimes necessary. Unexplained manual work should not become the permanent process.</p></div></div>
        <div class="principle"><span>02</span><div><h3>Document the why</h3><p>A command explains what changed. Good documentation preserves the reasoning and constraints behind it.</p></div></div>
        <div class="principle"><span>03</span><div><h3>Design for ownership</h3><p>The person operating a system tomorrow matters as much as the person implementing it today.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
